The preference for better-corroborated theories inevitably smuggles induction back in because acting on a theory implies an expectation that its past success predicts future reliability. If corroboration offers no probabilistic weight, preferring one theory over another for practical action is irrational unless one assumes the uniformity of nature (that the future will resemble the past). This view survives by accepting the cost that Popper's distinction between 'corroboration' and 'probability' collapses in practice, rendering his rejection of induction inconsistent with actual scientific methodology.
