Popper distinguishes between the logical probability of a theory (which corroboration does not increase) and the rational choice of which theory to act upon. The preference is defensible because it relies on the critical method: we choose the theory that has withstood the most severe tests not because we believe it is probably true or will remain true, but because it is currently the best-tested guide available. This is a decision based on past performance under scrutiny, not an inductive inference about the future. The cost of accepting this view is the abandonment of any desire for certainty or guaranteed success; we must accept that even the best-corroborated theory may fail tomorrow, and our choice is merely the most rational one possible in the absence of truth.
